UNIX_EXTRA(n)                 Ribbon OS                  UNIX_EXTRA(n)

NAME
    unix_extra - stream channels that keep passed fds, datagrams and
    peer credentials for Tcl

SYNOPSIS
    package require unix_extra 0.3

    unix_extra::connect path
    unix_extra::listen path handler
    unix_extra::adopt channel
    unix_extra::fds channel ?-all? ?-messages?
    unix_extra::handoff channel target ?data?
    unix_extra::takeover target ?dataVar?
    unix_extra::inheritfd channel
    unix_extra::fdopen fd
    unix_extra::sendfds channel channels ?data?
    unix_extra::recvfds channel ?maxbytes?
    unix_extra::peercred channel
    unix_extra::socketpair ?stream|dgram?
    unix_extra::dgram ?path?
    unix_extra::sendto channel path data
    unix_extra::recv channel
    unix_extra::notify state ?path?

DESCRIPTION
    A companion to unix_sockets (Cyan Ogilvie), which gives Tcl unix
    stream sockets (unix_sockets::listen, unix_sockets::connect). This
    package adds the rest of what Ribbon's daemons and helpers need.

    Stream sockets made here (connect, listen, socketpair, adopt, fdopen,
    takeover) are "unix_extra channels": a channel type of its own that
    never loses a file descriptor passed with SCM_RIGHTS, whatever Tcl
    reads (see FD PASSING), and that can be handed to another process
    whole (see HANDING A CONNECTION TO ANOTHER PROCESS). Datagram sockets and received descriptors are ordinary Tcl
    file channels. All of them work with gets, read, fileevent, fcopy,
    fconfigure (-blocking too) and close as usual. Every command that
    takes a channel works on any channel with a file descriptor:
    unix_sockets channels, Tcl's own file and pipe channels, and the
    channels made here (except fds, which needs a unix_extra channel or a
    unix_sockets channel opened with -passfds 1).

    A path that starts with "@" is an abstract socket name (Linux): the
    "@" becomes the leading NUL byte and no file is created. Data
    arguments and results are byte strings; use encoding convertto/from
    for text that isn't ASCII.

STREAM CHANNELS
    unix_extra::connect path
        Connect to a listening unix stream socket and return a unix_extra
        channel (uxstreamN). Blocks until connected; a missing socket is
        ENOENT, one nobody listens on ECONNREFUSED.

    unix_extra::listen path handler
        Listen on path and return the listening channel (uxlistenN; it can
        only be closed). Each connection is accepted as a unix_extra
        channel, and handler is called at global level with the channel's
        name appended, as unix_sockets::listen does. An error in handler
        is a background error and closes that channel. As with dgram, an
        existing file at path is an error (EADDRINUSE), and closing the
        listener removes the file.

    unix_extra::socketpair ?stream|dgram?
        Return two connected channels: unix_extra channels for stream (the
        default), file channels for dgram. Hand one to a child with
        exec ... <@ $chan >@ $chan, then close it in the parent.

    unix_extra::adopt channel
        Turn a connected unix stream socket channel into a unix_extra
        channel and return the new channel's name; the old name is gone.
        It takes a unix_sockets channel, or a Tcl file channel that fds or
        recvfds made for a passed socket (or stdin under inetd-style
        activation). A unix_extra channel is returned unchanged. It is safe
        at any point, from a unix_sockets accept handler too, and
        guarantees:
          - Output: everything written to the old channel is sent first
            (flushed, blocking if need be), so byte order is kept. If that
            flush fails, adopt fails and the old channel is left as it was.
          - Input: no byte is lost or reordered. Bytes Tcl had already read
            into the old channel's buffer move over raw and are the first
            the new channel returns. Stream offset 0 is the first byte not
            yet read through the old channel.
          - Queued fds: a unix_sockets channel opened with -passfds 1 has
            already taken the passed fds from the kernel. They move over
            in order, taken or not, with their offsets rebased: those of
            messages the script had read get offsets <= 0, and fds hands
            them out at once. (adopt finds them through the channel's
            read-only -fdqueue option and duplicates them, so the two
            packages don't link against each other.)
          - Options: -blocking, -buffering, -buffersize, -encoding,
            -eofchar and -translation carry over. Fileevent scripts do not:
            set them again on the new channel.
          - The old channel is closed. Before that, its descriptor is
            pointed at a dead socket (dup2), so if something else still
            holds it (an interp it was shared with, or unix_sockets while
            its accept handler runs), nothing can read from or write to the
            real socket through it again: reads see end of file and writes
            fail with EPIPE.
        Without -passfds, adopt can't bring back descriptors that came with
        bytes the old channel had already read, because the kernel
        discarded them at that read. Adopt such a channel before the peer
        can send descriptors, e.g. first thing in the accept handler, or
        open it with -passfds 1. A channel with a transform
        stacked on it is refused (STACKED), and so is anything that is not
        a connected unix stream socket (NOTSTREAM).

    Half-close works: close $chan write sends end of file to the peer,
    which can still answer; close $chan read makes the peer's writes fail.
    peercred, sendfds and recvfds work on these channels.

FD PASSING
    A descriptor sent with SCM_RIGHTS travels with the bytes of the
    sendmsg that carried it. The kernel hands it to the first read that
    takes some of those bytes. A plain read() can't take it, so the kernel
    closes it and it is lost. That is what Tcl's gets, read and fileevent
    do on a unix_sockets or file channel, including when Tcl reads ahead
    into its buffer.

    A unix_extra channel reads only with recvmsg, so it takes every
    descriptor. Each message's descriptors are queued on the channel, in
    order, tagged with the stream offset (bytes since the channel was
    made or adopted) where the message that carried them ended. This
    offset is exact, because the kernel always ends a read right after a
    message that carries descriptors. The one exception is a message
    larger than the channel's -buffersize (default 4096): its
    descriptors arrive with its first part, and the offset is where that
    part ended. Where a message began can't be known, because the read
    may have started with earlier bytes, and a MSG_PEEK looks past message
    ends for descriptors.

    So reading works as usual, and fds hands the descriptors out once the
    script has read their message. From then on fds (without -all) returns
    them, and it never returns those of a later message. A protocol that
    knows how many descriptors a message carries (Wayland, D-Bus) can just
    as well take them from the queue in order. A message counts as read
    only when all of its bytes are, so a one-byte sendfds message (the
    default NUL) must be read too.

        # sender
        puts $c "OPEN image.squashfs"; flush $c
        unix_extra::sendfds $c [list $f] "FD\n"
        # receiver, in its readable fileevent
        while {[gets $c line] >= 0} {
            if {$line eq "FD"} {set image [lindex [unix_extra::fds $c] 0]}
        }

    Descriptors still queued when the channel is closed are closed with
    it, so none leak.

    unix_extra::fds channel ?-all? ?-messages?
        Take descriptors queued on a unix_extra channel (or on a
        unix_sockets channel opened with -passfds 1: then this calls
        unix_sockets::fds, which works the same way) and return a new
        Tcl channel for each, in the order they were sent (close-on-exec,
        made as recvfds makes them). Without -all it takes those whose
        message the script has read. With -all it takes every one Tcl has
        received, including those of a message still in Tcl's buffer.
        With -messages the result is one {offset {channel ...}} pair per
        message instead of a flat list. It returns {} when nothing is
        queued, and it never blocks or reads. If the kernel could not pass
        all of a message's descriptors (the open file limit was reached
        when they arrived), the first fds call that reaches that message
        fails once with errorCode {UNIX_EXTRA CTRUNC}, and the next call
        returns those that did arrive.

    unix_extra::sendfds channel channels ?data?
        Send the file descriptors of the listed channels over a unix
        stream (or datagram) socket with SCM_RIGHTS, attached to data
        (default: one NUL byte; it must not be empty). Returns the number
        of bytes sent. Tcl's pending output on channel is flushed first,
        so byte order is kept. If a non-blocking channel can't flush it
        all, nothing is sent (POSIX EAGAIN; retry when it is writable).
        The sent channels stay open here; close them when done. At most
        253.

    unix_extra::recvfds channel ?maxbytes?
        The low-level form, for raw use on any socket channel. On a
        unix_extra channel, use fds instead. It receives with one recvmsg
        of at most maxbytes (default 4096) and returns {data {channel
        ...}}: the bytes and a new Tcl channel for each descriptor that
        arrived (close-on-exec set, readable and/or writable as the
        descriptor was opened). At end of stream it returns {{} {}}. The
        kernel stops a read right after the message that carries
        descriptors, so stream data sent later stays queued for normal
        Tcl reads.

        On channels of other types, call recvfds BEFORE reading that
        message through Tcl. The kernel has already discarded the
        descriptors of bytes Tcl has buffered, so recvfds refuses (errorCode
        UNIX_EXTRA BUFFERED) when the channel has buffered input. On a
        unix_extra channel recvfds also works. Its bytes count in the
        stream offsets, and it leaves the queue alone.

    Why a channel type and not a transform: a transform pushed onto a
    unix_sockets channel (Tcl_StackChannel) sees only the bytes that the
    channel below it read. That channel reads with read(), which has
    already made the kernel drop the descriptors, and no layer above the
    driver can get them back. The read itself has to be a recvmsg, so it
    has to be done by the driver: this one, or unix_sockets' own with
    the -passfds patch Ribbon carries (srcpkgs/tcl-unix-sockets/patches,
    offered upstream). Without that patch, adopting a channel onto this
    driver is the way.

    Limits: nothing else may read the socket. A child given it with exec
    reads with read() and loses descriptors, as always; to share a
    connection on purpose, hand it off (next section). Under a transform
    pushed onto a unix_extra channel, fds counts every byte the transform
    has taken as read. The kernel passes at most 253 descriptors per
    message.

HANDING A CONNECTION TO ANOTHER PROCESS
    unix_extra::handoff channel target ?data?
        Move the unix_extra channel, a live connection, to the process at
        the other end of target (a connected unix socket channel, normally
        a unix_extra channel from connect or socketpair). One message
        carries everything: the connection's socket, every fd still queued
        on it (taken or not, with their offsets), the bytes Tcl has read
        ahead that the script hasn't, the channel's mode and options
        (-blocking, -buffering, -buffersize, -encoding, -eofchar,
        -translation), and data (default: none). Output written to the
        channel is flushed to its peer first, as with adopt. Then the
        channel is closed here without shutting the socket down: the peer
        doesn't notice, it is just talking to the other process now. As
        with adopt, the old fd is first pointed at a dead socket, so an
        interp that still shares the channel can't touch the connection.
        Returns an empty string.

        It is atomic: if the send fails (the target's peer has gone, EPIPE;
        a full non-blocking target, EAGAIN; a target that isn't a socket),
        the channel is left exactly as it was: same unread bytes, same
        queue, same options. A non-blocking target that takes only part of
        the message is finished in blocking mode, because the fds went
        with that first part. A channel with more than 252 queued fds
        (NFDS) or a transform stacked on it (STACKED) is refused, and so
        is handing a channel off over itself (TARGET). Fileevent scripts
        don't travel.

        Success means the kernel has the message. If the receiver exits
        without taking it over, the kernel closes everything in it and the
        connection's peer sees end of stream.

    unix_extra::takeover target ?dataVar?
        Receive a connection sent with handoff over target (a unix_extra
        channel) and return it as a new unix_extra channel: the unread
        bytes come first, then the stream; the fd queue is as it was, with
        offsets relative to the first unread byte (fds hands out those of
        messages the sender's script had read at once); mode and options
        are the sender's. With dataVar, the data is stored there. It
        blocks until the whole message has arrived (it was sent in one
        piece), whatever target's -blocking, and it works whatever Tcl had
        already read ahead on target; other fds queued on target stay
        there. Not a handoff message, a version it doesn't know, or end of
        stream: errorCode {UNIX_EXTRA HANDOFF}; fds the kernel couldn't
        pass: {UNIX_EXTRA CTRUNC}.

    The wire format: one sendmsg, SCM_RIGHTS = the connection's socket,
    then each queued batch's fds in queue order, and the bytes (integers
    big-endian):
        0   "UXHO"
        4   u8 version (1)
        5   u8 flags: 1 readable, 2 writable
        6   u16 0
        8   u32 nmsg   queued batches
        12  u32 nopt   bytes of options, a Tcl list {name value ...}
        16  u32 nbuf   bytes read ahead and not read by the script
        20  u32 ndata  bytes of data
        24  nmsg x {i64 end, u32 nfd, u32 lost}, end relative to the
            first unread byte, then the options, unread bytes and data.
    The fds ride on the first bytes of the message, so takeover finds them
    as the first batch queued on target after the header's first byte.

    A receiver must take the message with takeover, or at least read it
    with recvmsg. A process that reads target with plain read() (a
    program that doesn't use unix_extra) loses every fd in the message,
    the connection itself included: the kernel closes them at that read.
    Nothing on the sending side can prevent that.

    Handing off to a child started with exec: make a socketpair, let the
    child inherit one end and nothing else, and hand off over the other:

        lassign [unix_extra::socketpair] here there
        set fd [unix_extra::inheritfd $there]  ;# close-on-exec off, this fd only
        exec tclsh worker.tcl $fd &
        close $there                           ;# the child has its own copy
        unix_extra::handoff $conn $here

        # worker.tcl
        package require unix_extra
        set t [unix_extra::fdopen [lindex $argv 0]]
        set conn [unix_extra::takeover $t]

    All other sockets made here stay close-on-exec, so the child gets
    only that one fd (and stdin, stdout, stderr, and whatever this
    process itself inherited without close-on-exec: exec does not
    close that, and neither does handoff, which starts no process). Close $there right
    after the exec: until then, any other child exec'd by this process
    (from another thread, say) inherits it too.

    unix_extra::inheritfd channel
        Clear close-on-exec on the channel's fd and return the fd number,
        for a child to pick up with fdopen.

    unix_extra::fdopen fd
        Make a unix_extra channel of an inherited fd: a connected unix
        stream socket (else NOTSTREAM; a bad fd is POSIX EBADF). It sets
        close-on-exec again, so the fd isn't passed further. An fd that
        already belongs to a channel of this interp is refused (INUSE), and
        so are 0, 1 and 2 (STDIO): use adopt on stdin, stdout or stderr.

PEER CREDENTIALS
    unix_extra::peercred channel
        Return {pid P uid U gid G} of the process at the other end of a
        connected unix socket (SO_PEERCRED: recorded when it connected,
        or for a socketpair, when the pair was made). Works on channels
        accepted by unix_extra::listen or unix_sockets::listen, which is
        how a root helper authorises its callers.

DATAGRAMS
    unix_extra::dgram ?path?
        Create a SOCK_DGRAM socket and return its channel. With path, bind
        it there; a bound path is removed when the channel is closed. An
        existing file at path is an error (EADDRINUSE): remove stale
        sockets first. Read datagrams with unix_extra::recv, not gets/read.

    unix_extra::sendto channel path data
        Send data as one datagram to path, or to the connected peer when
        path is "" (a socketpair end). Returns the number of bytes sent.

    unix_extra::recv channel
        Return the next datagram, whole, whatever its size. Blocks unless
        the channel is non-blocking; then an empty queue is a POSIX EAGAIN
        error. Use it from a readable fileevent.

    unix_extra::notify state ?path?
        sd_notify: send state (e.g. "READY=1", "STATUS=...") as one
        datagram to path, default $env(NOTIFY_SOCKET). Returns 1 when
        sent and 0 when NOTIFY_SOCKET is unset or empty.

ERRORS
    System call failures set errorCode to {POSIX ENAME message} and the
    result to "call: message". Usage errors set errorCode to
    {UNIX_EXTRA CODE} with CODE one of PATH, NOFD, NFDS, DATA, MAXBYTES,
    BUFFERED, CTRUNC, NOTUNIX, NOPEER, NOTSTREAM, STACKED, TARGET,
    HANDOFF, FDQUEUE, STDIO, INUSE. Errors from unix_sockets::fds (called
    by fds) keep their {UNIX_SOCKETS CODE}.

NOTES
    A passed descriptor shares its open file description with the
    sender, including O_NONBLOCK; a received channel starts in the
    matching -blocking mode.

EXAMPLES
    # A dinit/systemd-style service reports readiness
    unix_extra::notify READY=1

    # kitd: authorise each caller
    unix_extra::listen /run/kitd.sock {apply {c {
        set cred [unix_extra::peercred $c]
        if {[dict get $cred uid] != 0} { ... pkcheck --process [dict get $cred pid] ... }
    }}}

    # kitd: hand an opened file to the caller
    set f [open /var/lib/kit/image.squashfs r]
    unix_extra::sendfds $c [list $f] [binary format I 0]
    close $f

    # client side: read the status word, then take the file
    set c [unix_extra::connect /run/kitd.sock]
    ...
    binary scan [read $c 4] I status
    set image [lindex [unix_extra::fds $c] 0]

    # code that already uses unix_sockets
    unix_sockets::listen $path {apply {c {
        set c [unix_extra::adopt $c]
        ...
    }}}

    # or with the -passfds patch: no fd is lost before adopt either
    unix_sockets::listen $path {apply {c {
        chan event $c readable [list serve $c]  ;# unix_extra::fds $c works
    }}} -passfds 1

    # a supervisor passes an accepted client to a worker process
    unix_extra::handoff $client $worker [list session $id]

BUILD
    make [O=builddir] [TCL_CONFIG=/usr/lib/tclConfig.sh]
    make test [TESTFLAGS="-tmpdir DIR"] [UNIX_SOCKETS_DIR=dir-with-unix_sockets]

    The unix_sockets tests are skipped when it can't be loaded, and the
    passfds ones when it lacks -passfds (srcpkgs/tcl-unix-sockets/patches).
    make install DESTDIR=...

    Built against the Tcl stubs library (Tcl 8.6). Socket paths are at
    most 107 bytes, so tests need a short -tmpdir.

